# ext_server manifest — the policy for the task that mounts the root (#511).
#
# 🔴 FOUND THE SAME WAY default_pager's WAS, and it failed harder: the root
# filesystem is the thing the rest of the boot is standing on.
#
#	ext2: cap_request(ahci0a) failed (2413)
#	bootstrap: '.../ext_server' task terminated
#
# ⚠️ Shipped on BOTH targets since #498, by two different rules: i386's bundle
# is assembled by scripts/make-bundle.sh, x86-64's by servers/bootstrap's
# CMakeLists.txt, and the .cmf is named in each.  A manifest that exists in the
# export tree and is not in the bundle is a policy nobody reads, which is worse
# than none because it looks like one.

name ext_server

# RESOURCE_BLK_DEVICE (4), any device, READ|WRITE (0x3).
#
# ⚠️ `any', because which partition holds the root is a boot argument: it is
# `ahci0a' under one bootstrap.conf and something else under another.  This
# says the server may open a block device read-write; which one is the boot's
# decision, and the capability it is issued names the instance.
required 4 any 0x3

# RESOURCE_DMA_BUFFER (10), any buffer, the device may READ and WRITE it (0x3)
# (#599).
#
# The block server's disk fills this server's page cache by DMA, and on a
# machine that confines devices the kernel maps the cache for that disk only
# on a capability naming the buffer, issued to its owner and handed to the
# block server.  Without this line cap_server refuses the capability, the
# mount says so, and the cache copies instead.  `any', because the buffer is
# created at mount time; cap_server asks the kernel whose it is.
required 10 any 0x3

# RESOURCE_MOUNT_POINT (3), the four prefixes this server mounts, REGISTER
# (0x1) (#716).
#
# The name server checks a mount in only for a task that shows a capability
# for its prefix -- before, any task could register "/etc" and answer every
# open under it.  Named one by one and NOT `any', unlike the device above:
# which partition holds the root is the boot's to say, but where each one is
# mounted is written in ext_server.c, part_table[], and nowhere else.  A fifth
# row there needs a fifth line here, and without it the mount is refused with
# the reason printed -- a prefix this server could register without anyone
# writing it down would be the same hole with a narrower door.
required 3 name:/ 0x1
required 3 name:/mnt/disk1 0x1
required 3 name:/mnt/disk2 0x1
required 3 name:/mnt/disk3 0x1
